From: Core
Subject: [SECURITY] Outbound mail leaves this platform in plaintext

Gmail is telling our recipients "tatvaos.com did not encrypt this message",
and it is right. Every email TatvaOS sends to the outside world crosses the
internet unencrypted, readable by anything on the path.

WHY IT REACHED PRODUCTION. main.cf sets both TLS levels to `none` with the
comment "Mailpit speaks plain SMTP; no TLS locally" - correct for the local
stack, which relays everything to Mailpit. The entrypoint templates
myhostname, mydomain and relayhost from the environment for production, but
it never touches the TLS lines. So the local-only setting shipped verbatim to
a server doing direct MX delivery to Gmail.

THE FIX IS ONE WORD, AND IT IS SAFE IN BOTH ENVIRONMENTS. `may` is
opportunistic TLS: Postfix offers STARTTLS, uses it when the far end supports
it - which Gmail, Outlook and every major provider do - and falls back to
plaintext when it does not. Nothing stops being delivered. Mailpit, which
speaks plain SMTP, still works untouched.

NOT `encrypt`: that refuses to deliver to a server without TLS, which turns a
confidentiality improvement into silent bounces to small hosts. Opportunistic
is what the rest of the internet runs.

INBOUND (smtpd_tls_security_level) is deliberately NOT changed here. Offering
STARTTLS to senders needs a certificate for the MX name, which is a separate
job with its own failure modes - and leaving it at `none` today is no worse
than yesterday, whereas outbound is a live confidentiality problem with a
one-word fix.

--- a/local/postfix/main.cf
+++ b/local/postfix/main.cf
@@ -150,8 +150,26 @@
 relayhost = [mailpit]:1025

-# Mailpit speaks plain SMTP; no TLS locally
-smtp_tls_security_level  = none
+# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# TLS
+# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
+#  OUTBOUND: opportunistic. Postfix offers STARTTLS and uses it whenever the
+#  receiving server supports it, falling back to plaintext when it does not.
+#  No certificate is needed for this - we are the client.
+#
+#  This was `none` until 19 August 2026, which meant every message this
+#  platform sent crossed the internet in the clear. Gmail says so to the
+#  recipient, in red, under "security". The setting was written for the local
+#  stack (Mailpit speaks plain SMTP) and the entrypoint never overrode it for
+#  production, so a development convenience became a production exposure.
+#
+#  `may` and not `encrypt`: encrypt REFUSES delivery to a server with no TLS,
+#  which trades a confidentiality problem for undelivered mail to smaller
+#  hosts. Opportunistic is what the rest of the internet runs.
+#
+#  Safe locally too - Mailpit does not offer STARTTLS, so Postfix falls back
+#  and behaves exactly as before.
+smtp_tls_security_level  = may
+smtp_tls_loglevel        = 1
+
+#  INBOUND: unchanged. Offering STARTTLS to senders needs a certificate for
+#  the MX name; that is its own piece of work. Leaving this at `none` is no
+#  worse than yesterday, while outbound was actively leaking.
 smtpd_tls_security_level = none
